---
// Hattiesburg About page body. Rendered inside src/pages/about.astro.
// Facts: founded 1882 by William H. Hardy, named for his wife Hattie; New Orleans & Northeastern
// railroad through town in 1884; lumber boom from 1897; "Hub City" from a 1912 newspaper contest.
import { getAllProducts } from '../../lib/products';

const greetings = (await getAllProducts()).find(p => p.handle.startsWith('greetings-from-hattiesburg'));
---
        <div class="space-y-6 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal/90">
          <p>
            Hattiesburg was a railroad town from the start. William H. Hardy founded it in 1882 and named it
            for his wife, Hattie. The trains came through in 1884, the pine woods fed a lumber boom, and in 1912
            the town picked its own nickname in a newspaper contest: the Hub City.
          </p>

          <p>
            These designs come from postcards, photographs and old newspaper pages — the things that survived
            when the buildings didn't. Every piece starts with a place people in Hattiesburg still talk about.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is Hattiesburg the way the people who grew up there remember it: Saturday afternoons on Main
            Street, Hardy Street, Palmers Crossing, and a postcard that said "Greetings from Hattiesburg" from
            the middle of a pansy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="mt-16 mx-auto max-w-[60%]">
          {greetings ? (
            <a href={`/products/${greetings.handle}`} class="block transition-transform duration-300 hover:scale-[1.02]">
              <img
                src="/images/hattiesburg/greetings_web.jpg"
                alt="Early 1900s postcard reading Greetings from Hattiesburg, Miss., with five small scenes set in a pansy"
                class="w-full h-auto rounded-sm shadow-[0_4px_12px_rgba(0,0,0,0.15)] border border-light-gray/30"
              />
            </a>
          ) : (
            <img
              src="/images/hattiesburg/greetings_web.jpg"
              alt="Early 1900s postcard reading Greetings from Hattiesburg, Miss., with five small scenes set in a pansy"
              class="w-full h-auto rounded-sm shadow-[0_4px_12px_rgba(0,0,0,0.15)] border border-light-gray/30"
            />
          )}
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-xs text-warm-gray text-center">
            Postcard, early 1900s. Mississippi Department of Archives and History, Cooper Postcard Collection.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
